, "alarm_features": ["Fever (99.5°F / 37.5°C)", "Unilateral purulent nasal discharge with blood", "Worsening pain not responding to initial treatment", "History of chronic underlying condition (asthma)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Infectious / Inflammatory Upper Airway Disorder", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.7988165680473371, "danger": 0.1, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Although the patient has a fever and a chronic underlying condition, these findings are not sufficient on their own to support a neoplastic or malignant nasal …"], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B1.4"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.2, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Foreign Body Involving Nasal Passage", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.21113689095127613, "posterior": 0.1775147928994083, "danger": 0.3, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["The findings do not directly contradict the presence of a foreign body, but the lack of reported history of insertion or a long-standing unilateral obstruction…"], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Neoplastic / Malignant Nasal/Paranasal Process", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.004, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Although the patient has a fever and a chronic underlying condition, these findings are not sufficient on their own to support a neoplastic or malignant nasal …"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.001, "danger": 0.1, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Traumatic / Vascular Nasal Lesion", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.018, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The unilateral purulent nasal discharge with blood that persists despite decongestant treatment is a feature commonly seen in infectious or inflammatory upper …"]}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Acute Bacterial Sinusitis with Complication", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.4117611175501738, "posterior": 0.4117611175501738, "danger": 0.2, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Viral Upper Respiratory Infection with Secondary Bacterial Superinfection", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.24705667053010427, "posterior": 0.24705667053010427, "danger": 0.1, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.1, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (GPA) or Other Vasculitis", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.09882266821204172, "posterior": 0.09882266821204172, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.0, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.4": {"id": "B1.4", "label": "Fungal Sinusitis (e.g., Rhinocerebral Mucormycosis)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.04117611175501738, "posterior": 0.04117611175501738, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.0, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B1.4", "B2"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the unilateral purulent nasal discharge with blood and persistence despite initial decongestant treatment provide strong support for an infectious or inflammatory upper airway disorder?", "summary": "The unilateral purulent nasal discharge with blood that persists despite decongestant treatment is a feature commonly seen in infectious or inflammatory upper airway disorders, such as bacterial sinusitis. This supports the B1 branch."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the presence of fever, unilateral purulent discharge with blood, and a chronic underlying condition sufficient to support a neoplastic or malignant nasal process?", "summary": "Although the patient has a fever and a chronic underlying condition, these findings are not sufficient on their own to support a neoplastic or malignant nasal process. The evidence is strong against malignancy, particularly given the age and lack of other constitutional symptoms."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is there any evidence in the case that directly contradicts the presence of a foreign body in the nasal passage?", "summary": "The findings do not directly contradict the presence of a foreign body, but the lack of reported history of insertion or a long-standing unilateral obstruction weakens this hypothesis. The evidence remains weak for B2."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Infectious / Inflammatory Upper Airway Disorder? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "summary": "The unilateral purulent discharge mixed with blood, along with fever and persistence despite initial treatment, is consistent with an infectious process. No single strong finding in the current evidence is inconsistent with this diagnosis."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.799], ["B1.1", 0.412], ["B1.2", 0.247]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "The leading hypothesis (B1.1: Acute Bacterial Sinusitis with Complication) has a posterior of 0.412, which is not yet >= 0.75. Additionally, several dangerous alternatives (e.g., B1.3 and B1.4) remain unresolved and have non-negligible posteriors. Further refinement is necessary to increase diagnostic confidence and rule out high-risk conditions."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.8, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.4117611175501738, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Bleeding and infected vessel of Kiesselbach plexus"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Foreign body obstruction"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Nasopharyngeal carcinoma"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Septal perforation"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Sinusitis with bacterial superinfection"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Upper airway obstruction', 'Frequent upper respiratory infections'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.799}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.1", "label": "Acute Bacterial Sinusitis with Complication", "posterior": 0.412, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1.2", "B2", "B1.3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=80%, source=both]\n\nForeign Body Involving Nasal Passage vs Acute Bacterial Sinusitis with Complication:\n  Favours first:  coughing, difficulty breathing, ear pain, facial pain, nasal bleeding\n  Favours second: bad breath, cough, ear pressure, facial pain or pressure, fatigue\n\nForeign Body Involving Nasal Passage vs Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (GPA) or Other Vasculitis:\n  Favours first:  coughing, difficulty breathing, ear pain, facial pain, nasal bleeding\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal oral cavity morphology, abnormal pericardium morphology, abnormal pleura morphology, abnormality of the hypothalamus-pituitary axis\n\nForeign Body Involving Nasal Passage vs Fungal Sinusitis (e.g., Rhinocerebral Mucormycosis):\n  Favours first:  coughing, difficulty breathing, ear pain, facial pain, nasal bleeding\n  Favours second: abnormal basal ganglia mri signal intensity, abnormal central motor function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal ekg, abnormal growth hormone level\n\nAcute Bacterial Sinusitis with Complication vs Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (GPA) or Other Vasculitis:\n  Favours first:  bad breath, ear pressure, facial pain or pressure, hyposmia, nasal congestion\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal oral cavity morphology, abnormal pericardium morphology, abnormal pleura morphology, abnormality of the hypothalamus-pituitary axis\n\nAcute Bacterial Sinusitis with Complication vs Fungal Sinusitis (e.g., Rhinocerebral Mucormycosis):\n  Favours first:  bad breath, ear pressure, hyposmia, purulent nasal discharge, toothache\n  Favours second: abnormal basal ganglia mri signal intensity, abnormal central motor function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal ekg, abnormal growth hormone level\n\nGranulomatosis with Polyangiitis (GPA) or Other Vasculitis vs Fungal Sinusitis (e.g., Rhinocerebral Mucormycosis):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal oral cavity morphology, abnormal pericardium morphology, abnormal pleura morphology, abnormality of the hypothalamus-pituitary axis\n  Favours second: abnormal basal ganglia mri signal intensity, abnormal central motor function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal ekg, abnormal growth hormone level\n\nRelated to Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (GPA) or Other Vasculitis: anti-neutrophil antibody associated vasculitis, arteritis, central nervous system vasculitis\n\nRelated to Fungal Sinusitis (e.g., Rhinocerebral Mucormycosis): chronic rhinosinusitis, ethmoid sinusitis, frontal sinusitis\n\n[RAG context for 'Initially' (score=0.59):]\n  Source: Clinical practice guideline (update): adult sinusitis. > Continuing Education Activity\n  Sinusitis is one of the most frequently encountered conditions in the primary care setting. This condition results from inflammation and blockage of the nasal passages or sinuses, caused by viral, bac\n[RAG context for 'Initially' (score=0.59):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  A small proportion of viral upper respiratory tract infections are complicated by bacterial sinusitis. Cultures of sinus puncture aspirates show that M. catarrhalis accounts for ~20% of cases of acute\n[RAG context for 'the pediatrician prescribed decongestants and sent the patient home' (score=0.53):]\n  Source: Clinical practice guideline (update): adult sinusitis. > History and Physical\n  Children show slight differences in the clinical signs of acute bacterial rhinosinusitis. Besides the 10-day duration, key symptoms, and double worsening, children are more prone to have fevers. Initi\n[RAG context for 'the pediatrician prescribed decongestants and sent the patient home' (score=0.56):]\n  Source: Clinical practice guideline (update): adult sinusitis. > Continuing Education Activity\n  Sinusitis is one of the most frequently encountered conditions in the primary care setting. This condition results from inflammation and blockage of the nasal passages or sinuses, caused by viral, bac\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  Initially the pediatrician prescribed decongestants and sent the patient home; since then, the patient has developed a nasal discharge with worsening pain → Delayed progression of sinusitis → Acute Bacterial Sinusitis with Complication\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Look for evidence of secondary complications (e.g., orbital cellulitis, CNS involvement) to confirm the presence of a bacterial complication in sinusitis\n  The patient has a medical history of asthma which is well-controlled with albuterol → Increased susceptibility to upper respiratory infections or secondary complications → Viral Upper Respiratory Infection with Secondary Bacterial Superinfection\n    (frequency: frequent)"}
